      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, thanks for clarifying. So you're saying the risk is tied to the socioeconomics. \n\nBut if we're looking at the \"risk\" of decline or crime-related issues, a lot of the predictive modeling used in urban planning relies on historical crime statistics. Statistically, certain demographics are often correlated with higher crime rates in these urban centers. \n\nIn your assessment, if a district has a high concentration of Black or Latino residents, how much does that statistically influence your \"instability\" forecast compared to a district with a majority White population, even if the income levels were similar? I'm trying to see if the demographic makeup serves as a reliable proxy for risk in your analysis.",
